**Verdict — speculative assessment.** Even granting the strongest GRH in the question, I find no new factoring exponent. The clearest gain is under ordinary RH alone: the project’s genus‑2 algorithm loses its RH assumption. Dirichlet GRH makes the final deterministic split effective once suitable relations are supplied. Number-field GRH makes square certification cheaper. The project still lacks a deterministic relation constructor, a rigorous \(L[1/3]\) factorization, and a construction below \(L[1/3]\). [Genus‑2 §0](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genus2rh_results.md:7) · [DZ §5.1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/zroute_results.md:379) · [Nested §§7–8](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nested_results.md:424) · [Frontier audit §5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/completeness_audit_results.md:178)

### Q1. Which hypotheses disappear?

- **Proved internally, given RH for \(\zeta\).** Genus‑2 Theorem R finds a proper divisor in expected \(L_p[1/2,2+o(1)](\log N)^{O(1)}\), where \(p\) is the least prime factor. For \(p\le N^\beta\), the constant in \(L_N[1/2]\) is \(2\sqrt\beta\). RH enters through smooth-number density in intervals of length \(x^{3/4}\). The constant 2 uses Younis’s Theorem 1.3 and the project’s unrefereed algorithmic accounting. The written alternative gives \(3/\sqrt2\). [Theorem R and alternatives §§0, 4, 7.1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genus2rh_results.md:7) · [Younis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05761)
- **KNOWN and proved internally, given Dirichlet GRH.** This covers “ERH” in Miller–Bach and DZ. After trial division, primes below \(2\log^2N\) generate the unit group. This removes the small-base assumption in Propositions Z, EQ and PIN. Proposition GX also gives every unit class a smooth representative \(\le N^{2+\varepsilon}\) at the stated \(L[\alpha]\) smoothness bound. DSR’s randomized constructor, almost-all-target estimate and Gaussian-set Fourier obstruction lose their GRH qualifier. Proposition ZA still uses randomized supply and Theorem A’s internal chain. Further consequences include one-sided catalogue abundance, the genus-membership/QR reduction, and small twist lists for the stated CM trace oracles. Each retains its catalogue, input or oracle restrictions. [detGRH §§1–5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/detGRH_results.md:138) · [ZA](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/zroute_results.md:275) · [GX](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/zroute_results.md:423) · [DSR verdict](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dsr_results.md:25) · [AB′](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonexact_results.md:235) · [GEN](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genusleak_results.md:159) · [CM twists](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:238)
- **Proved internally, given Dedekind GRH.** Lemma \(X_{\rm ERH}\) needs \(\zeta_K\) and **every** \(\zeta_{K(\sqrt h)}\) for the nontrivial square classes under test. Effective Chebotarev, uniform in degree and discriminant and excluding bad primes, gives detection probability at least \(1/(5d)\), polynomial-bit character primes and no exceptional class. Fixed-degree splitting-field GRH also supplies a splitting prime for square-root extraction. [Nested §7](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nested_results.md:424) · [detGRH §5, rows 8–9](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/detGRH_results.md:391)
- **KNOWN or proved internally, given Hecke GRH.** Class-character GRH gives Bach’s small class-group generators and the randomized reduction from factoring to class-group square roots. The broader Hecke hypothesis for the Arakelov ray-class walk gives the de Boer–Pellet-Mary–Wesolowski one-sided ideal sampler, including for adaptively chosen inputs under its density conditions. Its norm scale and the need for two compatible smooth cofactors persist. All-field Dedekind GRH covers finite-order abelian characters through class fields, including Dirichlet characters through cyclotomic fields. For the full walk one must check the infinite-place characters too. **Automorphic GRH** supplies the preceding cases to the extent its theorem includes the relevant \(\mathrm{GL}_1\) functions over the requisite base fields. I found no further factoring exponent that uses higher rank. [Nested §§1–3, 7](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nested_results.md:151) · [Class-group reduction §2.2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genusleak_results.md:137) · [Escape analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/escapeR2_results.md:264)

### Q2. Deterministic complete factoring

- **KNOWN worst case; proved internally on average.** The answer remains Harvey–Hittmeir’s \(N^{1/5+o(1)}\) on every input. Theorem AV gives the same deterministic algorithmic guarantee together with \(N^{1/6}\operatorname{polylog}N\) average time over its balanced-semiprime distribution. [Harvey–Hittmeir](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11105) · [AV §3, exact bounds and distribution](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/avgtime_results.md:273)
- **Proved implication; DSR open.** PIN needs the actual small-prime coordinate vectors in the **rational span** of valid integer relations. The pivot minor then yields a multiple of \(\lambda(N)\), and Miller splits deterministically. DZ forces that rank if, for every prescribed \(q^H\bmod N\), a deterministic algorithm produces the bounded, factored smooth representation within budget. With Dirichlet GRH granted, \(\mathrm{DSR}_\alpha\) alone gives deterministic complete \(L[\alpha,O(1)]\), including \(\alpha=1/2,2/5\). GX proves existence. Searching its witnesses is precisely the missing algorithm. [PIN](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/zroute_results.md:211) · [DZ and GX](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/zroute_results.md:379) · [DSR obstructions](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dsr_results.md:25)

### Q3. Rigorous randomized factoring

- **Proved internally, subject to audit.** U‑MF already claims \(L[2/5]\) using interval density and published inputs. The older FM and LD* are relation-count moments and remain hypotheses in the Lean assembly. R2’s \(2/5\) floor assumes its size/counting model **and forbids algebraic children**. Two-level descent uses such children. RAT and AB₂ address the rational and algebraic parts of \(C_1,C_2\) in the specified design. The claimed window is \(3/8<\alpha<2/5\), with \(a>2c_2\) at \(3/8\). The parent-filter repair and outstanding \(U'\)-under-DW write-up matter under every GRH. [U‑MF §0](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/mfU_theorem.md:14) · [Lean hypotheses](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/leanA_results.md:10) · [R2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nested_results.md:332) · [AB₂ §0](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:31) · [Audit and published-input update](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/STATUS.md)
- **Proved reductions; endpoints open.** Below \(3/8\), GRH supplies no required deeper conditional covariance. At \(1/3\), nested NR still needs an admissible sampler, **AD** (with probability at least half, factor exit or simultaneous two-sided supply for every target), and growing-degree **LV6′** bookkeeping. For randomized NFS, Lee–Venkatesan already obtain a congruence of squares. Proving it splits \(N\) still needs fruitfulness, e.g. Conjecture F, or a budgeted descent plus absorption. [NR §8](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nested_results.md:467) · [Deeper precision §7.5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/pc3_results.md:348) · [Fruitfulness alternatives](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/fruit_results.md:71) · [Lee–Venkatesan](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.08873)

### Q4. Below \(L[1/3]\) and the proposed method

- **Heuristic frontier.** The sharpest opening is a frame-less type‑D family of relative rank \(\ge3\) over a fixed small field, with non-power generators. A useful failure of \(G_r\) must produce enough independent small-norm relations at low construction cost. Conjugate copies already exhibit dependence and yield trivial gcds. Full favourable failure gives \(L[1/4]\) **in the typical-size model**. Theorem SS constrains single-family sampling. Prop SI needs cheap *factored outputs* near \(kN\), residue smoothness and fruitful rank, for a best modeled \(L[1/3,1.2114+\varepsilon]\). GRH settles neither the single non-CM coefficient \(a_f(N)\) nor deterministic nested PRIM gates, though it sharpens PRIM’s fixed-base exceptional-prime estimate. [grdeg §0](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/grdeg_results.md:19) · [midk §0](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/midk_results.md) · [SS §1.2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:157) · [SI requirements](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/sicheck_results.md:536) · [Constructor costs](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/siconstruct_results.md:281) · [Single coefficient](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/cuspcoef_results.md) · [PRIM](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/prim_results.md:338)
- **Speculative conversion.** The best match is the remark’s variance positivity applied to **incidence-weighted smooth pairs in progressions**. At three-level depth two, target relative child-count variance \(\phi_2\lesssim\eta_2\) and parent-count variance \(\sigma_2^2\phi_2\lesssim\eta_2^2\), with \(\eta_2\asymp D^{-2\alpha}\), \(D=\log N/\log\log N,\ \alpha=4/11\), after root and field conditioning. The endpoint needs constants and logarithmic losses controlled. The sketch treats von Mangoldt weights. It gives no identity inserting smoothness indicators or controlling the principal covariance, no constructor in intervals of length \(L[2/3]\) near \(kN\), no shifted character-sum estimate beating Burgess in the needed range, and no linked-module count. A \(\sqrt X\)-lift argument could prove an estimate offline. Executing that chain per input at \(X\asymp N\) would exhaust the budget. [Remark pp. 1, 3–4](https://alpo.ge/remark.pdf) · [Precision target](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/pc3_results.md:348) · [Phase-discarding barrier](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/cov38b_results.md:305)

### Q5. Ranked next threads

**Speculative estimates** are my probabilities of reaching the stated target within the indicated effort.

1. **Consolidate \(3/8\).** Prove \(U'\) under DW and integrate the repaired parent filter into one referee-ready argument. Independent of the remark. **60%, 2–4 researcher-weeks.** [Audit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/audit2L_audit.md) · [2L‑MF §5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/mf2L_results.md:392)
2. **Test the \(4/11\) variance target above.** The remark suggests a positivity argument only if its test weights can be transferred. **5% for the estimate, 4–8 weeks.** [Depth analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/pc3_results.md:348)
3. **Seek an explicit useful type‑D family.** Require growing rank, cheap linking and a box count of independent relations outside lower-rank sublattices. This is the direct sub‑\(1/3\) bet. The remark supplies no construction. **Under 1%, 1–3 months.** [Relative-resultant obstruction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/grdeg_results.md:128) · [Completeness audit §5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/completeness_audit_results.md:178)

### Q6. Credibility hygiene

- **KNOWN scope of the announcement.** The PDF claims RH and unspecified GRH and describes ERH as future work. I checked the PDF and did not validate the archive. Keep every existing status label pending independent verification. [Remark p. 1](https://alpo.ge/remark.pdf)
- **Proposed acceptance test.** Pin archive hashes, dependencies and toolchain. Independently state the challenge using standard analytic continuations: for **every** conductor, primitive Dirichlet character \(\chi\), and complex \(s\), \(0<\Re s<1\) and \(L(s,\chi)=0\) imply \(\Re s=1/2\). Check \(\zeta\) separately or via conductor one. Repeat with every required number field and Hecke character, including the infinity types used. Rebuild from source in isolation, inspect theorem parameters for hidden hypotheses such as weakened Vandiver, run `#print axioms`, and replay against the independent statement with a trusted kernel. Finally match Bach’s explicit cutoff and Chebotarev’s uniform degree/discriminant error to their published hypotheses. A clean axiom report alone can certify a conditional implication. The repository’s own Lean proof illustrates exactly this distinction. [Lean validation guidance](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/Interacting-with-Lean/) · [Project challenge and checks](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/leanA_results.md:10)